    {
      "id": "5v5enb0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Mountains and Hills of Gujarat"
    },
    {
      "id": "ywqjtma",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Aravalli Range: North-eastern Gujarat; includes Taranga and Gabbar hills",
        "Vindhya Range: Eastern Gujarat; forms natural boundary",
        "Girnar Hills (Junagarh): Highest peak in Gujarat — Mt. Gorakhnath (1,117 m). Sacred Jain and Hindu site.",
        "Satpura Range: South-eastern Gujarat; extends through Dang district",
        "Pavagadh Hill (Vadodara): UNESCO World Heritage Site with Champaner at its base; Kalika Mata Temple on summit",
        "Saputara (Dang district): Only hill station in Gujarat; elevation about 1,000 m",
        "Chotila: Famous for Chamunda Mata temple; in Surendranagar district"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "r8blrzv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2.1.4 Rann of Kutch"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9tyaoh5",
      "content": "The Rann of Kutch is a vast salt marsh in the Thar Desert in the Kutch district of Gujarat. It is one of the largest salt deserts in the world."
    },
    {
      "id": "41llch4",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Great Rann of Kutch: Lies to the north of Kutch; a large seasonal marsh.",
        "Little Rann of Kutch: Lies to the east of Kutch; also a seasonal salt marsh and home to the Indian Wild Ass Sanctuary (one of the last habitats for the Indian wild ass / Khur).",
        "Rann Utsav: Annual cultural festival held in Kutch (Nov–Feb), promoting tourism in the region.",
        "Kala Dungar (Black Hill): Highest point in Kutch (462 m). India's Passport is sometimes mentioned due to unique rock formation called 'Sunset Point.'"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kcd12kv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.2 Climate of Gujarat"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "24lqh5h",
      "content": "Gujarat has a tropical climate. The climate varies from arid in the west (Kutch) to humid in the south and east."
    },
    {
      "id": "x9n7pyx",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Season</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Months</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Characteristics</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Summer</p>",
            "<p>March to June</p>",
            "<p>Hot and dry; temperatures can exceed 45°C in Kutch</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Monsoon</p>",
            "<p>June to September</p>",
            "<p>SW monsoon brings rain; Dang gets highest rainfall (~2,000 mm)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Post-Monsoon</p>",
            "<p>October–November</p>",
            "<p>Moderate temperatures; retreating monsoon</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Winter</p>",
            "<p>November to February</p>",
            "<p>Cool; temperatures may drop to 5°C in Kutch and North Gujarat</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
